Three exterior edges at order thirty-six
========================================

This working argument concerns the existing lower bound n(4,6,10) >= 39
in Table 2, physical page 18, of the fixed source. No graph enumeration
or exclusion has yet been completed for this parameter.

The two depth-two Moore trees about a fixed edge contain twenty-six
vertices. Ten cross edges join their opposite leaves. Each side therefore
has seventeen missing leaf incidences. If the exterior has t vertices,
its degree sum gives 2e(Y) = 4t - 34. Hence n >= 35. Hexagon counting gives
10n/3 hexagons, so 3 divides n. Only n = 36 remains below the desired bound.
At that order the exterior has ten vertices and three edges.

Since the graph has girth six, these three edges form a forest. Up to
isomorphism its nontrivial components are exactly three independent edges,
a path of two edges and an independent edge, a path of three edges, or a
three-edge star. Isolated vertices fill the remaining positions.

Let h_i be the exterior degree of vertex i and let a_i be its number of
old neighbours on the left. Its number on the right is 4-h_i-a_i. Every
outside vertex has at most one old neighbour in each of the six branches;
otherwise a quadrangle results. In particular both side degrees lie
between zero and three. If ij is an exterior edge, their old branch sets
are disjoint. A common old neighbour gives a triangle, while different
neighbours in one branch give a pentagon. Thus

  a_i+a_j <= 3,   (4-h_i-a_i)+(4-h_j-a_j) <= 3.

More generally, two vertices of one exterior component cannot share an
old neighbour: their exterior distance is at most three, and adjoining
the two old incidences gives a cycle of length at most five. They may
share a branch through different old leaves when their distance is two
or three; excluding that would be an unjustified restriction.

For a list P of missing leaf degrees put f(P) = (51-sum(s*s for s in P))/2.
The acquired root equations give F_L=f(P_R) and F_R=f(P_L), where F_L and
F_R are the totals of unordered same-side neighbour pairs supplied by
outside vertices. This argument does not use independence of the exterior:
a hexagon through a pair of root branches has its four-edge path through
a single common neighbour of the two leaves. An exterior edge cannot
occur in that path.

As sum(a_i)=17, sum(h_i)=6, and there are ten exterior vertices, subtraction
of the two pair totals gives

  F_L-F_R = 12 - (sum(h_i*h_i))/2 - sum(h_i*a_i).

For every exterior edge ij, its contribution to the last sum is a_i+a_j.
In an independent-edge component the endpoint old degrees are both three,
so the displayed adjacent inequalities force a_i+a_j=3. For a path of two
edges its two edge sums are each two or three; the independent edge has
sum three. For a path of three edges the two end sums are two or three,
and the middle sum is one, two or three. For a star every sum is one, two
or three. Consequently the absolute profile difference is at most zero,
one, two or three, respectively.

The direct integer check boundary_profiles.py lists all side-degree
vectors satisfying these necessary conditions. It finds 1,216, 2,136,
3,738 and 5,814 vectors for the four labelled representatives. It does
not assert that any vector has a graph realization. The profile pairs
are sufficient for safe rejection of other roots, but are not sufficient
for accepting any root.

The existing matching-forest normal form gives a small root inventory:
18,351 literal deficit matrices, 310 matrix orbits, and 3,565,944 matching
products. This has been calculated without running the graph traversal.

The boundary needs a new complete constructor. If b is the number of
positive left-neighbour parts and F their pair total, then

  n_3=F-17+b,   n_2=51-2F-3b,   n_1=3b+F-34.

Here 6 <= b <= 10; append 10-b empty parts. For each exterior forest H,
vertex i has right capacity 4-deg_H(i)-|B_i|. Every zero part must occur
at a vertex of positive exterior degree. Trying every forest on these
literal labels, installing its edges, and then every right incidence
would be complete, provided the capacities and root equations are checked
and only necessary short-cycle tests are applied. There are 14,070
three-edge forests on ten labelled vertices, so applying that whole list
independently to every left partition may be wasteful. A more economical
assignment by the four forest types must prove its coverage and account
for empty labels and automorphisms before being used.

The present result is this analytic reduction and integer inventory only.
The root traversal, complete boundary, finite record and distinct review
are still outstanding. No lower-bound clause is credited.

An alternative complete parameterization
---------------------------------------

One can avoid choosing 14,070 edge sets after each left partition by fixing
one of the four exterior forests first. Assign its ten vertices subsets
B_i of the nine left leaves. A subset has at most one leaf in each left
branch, and its size lies between max(0,1-h_i) and min(3,4-h_i).
Its complementary right capacity is 4-h_i-|B_i|. Each leaf must occur in
exactly its prescribed number of subsets. The individual root-pair
equations remain exact constraints on these subsets.

Use any fixed total order of subsets, for example their integer bit masks.
For the matching, orient each edge so that its two subsets are increasing,
and sort the three resulting ordered pairs. For the path of two edges
and a separate edge, sort the two path-end subsets and orient the separate
edge increasingly. For the four-vertex path, require its sequence of
subsets to be lexicographically no greater than the reversed sequence.
For the star, sort the three leaf subsets. In every case also sort the
subsets at isolated exterior vertices. Ties are allowed.

These restrictions preserve coverage. Every automorphism of the chosen
forest is generated by exactly the stated edge reversals, equal-component
permutations, path reversal, star-leaf permutations and isolated-vertex
permutations. Relabel any hypothetical graph by such automorphisms until
its left subsets satisfy the restrictions. This relabelling also acts on
its right attachments; all right attachments for the surviving labels
must still be visited. Thus one must not impose an additional ordering
on right subsets merely because some left subsets are equal.

For a retained left assignment, install its literal edges and the fixed
forest, test girth, and run the acquired complete right-incidence visitor
with these ten capacities. The edge test by distance at least five remains
valid. At the end all vertices and edges of the thirty-six-vertex graph
are specified. This supplies a complete analytic parameterization, but
its implementation, finite controls and performance have not been tested.
In particular no reduction in running time is claimed.

Exact pruning in the implementation
-----------------------------------

The order of the exterior vertices is fixed before recursion. Let k_i=4-h_i.
For each suffix of that order, precompute the set of triples

  (sum a_i, sum binom(a_i,2), sum binom(k_i-a_i,2))

over all choices max(0,k_i-3)<=a_i<=min(3,k_i). At a partial assignment,
the first coordinate required of this suffix is the sum of the unused left
leaf capacities. The second is the sum of the unsupplied left root-pair
targets. The third is the full right pair target minus the pair totals
already forced by right capacities of the assigned vertices. Every graph
completion supplies precisely these three sums. Their absence from the
suffix set is therefore a necessary obstruction. Presence is not an
existence test: the degree inventory deliberately ignores geometry and
the individual three pair targets.

The other tests also reject only impossible extensions. A leaf whose
capacity is exhausted cannot receive another edge. Two neighbours of one
exterior vertex cannot occupy the same old rooted branch, since that would
give a quadrangle. A proposed edge whose endpoints are joined by a path of
length at most four creates a cycle of length at most five. All neighbour
pair contributions are nonnegative, so an individual pair total cannot
exceed its final target. The partial normalization tests compare only
already assigned masks; every completely normalized assignment satisfies
each such comparison on every prefix. These observations remain valid in
the presence of the fixed exterior edges.

After the left assignment, list every missing right incidence in increasing
leaf order. For repeated incidences of a single leaf, choose its exterior
neighbours in strictly increasing label order. This removes only the order
of its indistinguishable incidences, not any subset of neighbours. Apply the
same capacity, branch, pair-total and distance tests. At a terminal node all
vertices have degree four and all edges of the candidate are specified.
Counting simple paths of length five between each adjacent pair then tests
the hexagon condition exactly; every such path with the edge gives one
unoriented hexagon through that edge. There is no further completion stage.

The first implementation controls retain one known order-32 graph with
lambda=18 under the same matching constructor, including an explicit
adjacency-preserving map to the accepted model. A separate comparison
enumerates all three-colour assignments on each ten-vertex forest, obtains
their orbits from actual graph automorphisms, and checks the implementation's
representatives. Independent Cartesian degree enumeration checks every
suffix-budget set. These finite checks supplement the preceding coverage
proof; they do not prove a target exclusion.
